package scalar_facts

// Internal opt-in owned result for one source document holding a set of
// ordinary pure functions over numbers, bool, strings, and records and
// unions of numbers and bool, which may recurse. A function's body is
// locals, assignments, asserts, if statements, switches over integers or a
// union's variants, while loops, loops over int ranges, breaks, continues and
// returns. Values and conditions are literals, variables, arithmetic,
// shifts, conversions, comparisons, logic, record literals, field reads,
// union variants, their tests and payloads, a string's byte count, bytes and
// slices, and direct calls of functions of the set, whose operands are such
// expressions.
// This is not a general IR, and no compiler-private identity crosses this
// boundary.
//
// The compiler host passes arrays of flat records, so a module is one array
// of rows. Row 0 is its Source: `name` is the path and `text` the document.
// A rejected module has exactly one more row, its Rejection: `text` is the
// diagnostic's message and the range is its source. Nothing else survives
// a rejection, and it must never be emitted.
//
// An accepted module's row 1 is its Document: `name` is the shared source
// snapshot's document key, and `text` is empty for a package and the library's
// name for a native library source. Then each record and union the source declares
// follows in declaration order:
// - a record is a Record row, its `name`, `count` fields and declaration
//   range, then `count` Member rows: `name`, `type`, ordinal `value`
//   and name range. A member's `type` is a scalar, a string, an array, or
//   a record or union of the module, though not one that holds itself by
//   value, as an array holds its elements apart;
// - a union is a Union row, its `name`, `count` variants and declaration
//   range, then each variant in order: a Variant row, its `name`, ordinal
//   `value`, `count` payload fields and range, then `count` Member rows:
//   `name`, `type` as a record's field has, name range and `value`, the payload's ordinal
//   among all the union's payloads in declaration order.
// Then, for each function with several named results in declaration order,
// a record of them: a Record row named for the function with `.results`, its
// declaration's range, then a Member row for each result as for a field.
// The d-th of them is type RecordType + d, which a function's result, a
// parameter, a variable and an operand may have. Then each function follows
// in declaration order; a package's first is the module's entry and takes no
// parameters and returns int, and a library has no entry:
// - a Function row: its `name`, result `type`, `count` parameters and
//   declaration range, and in a library its stable symbol ID as `literal`,
//   the ID the runtime binds it by;
// - `count` Parameter rows: `name`, `type`, ordinal `value`, name range;
// - a Local row for each named result, with its name's range, whose operand
//   is its zero: a Literal, or a Zero for a record. A function with one
//   named result returns its value, and one with several the record of
//   them. A return of one named result's value returns it. A return of
//   several values is an Assign row for each, with the value's range, in
//   order, then a Return of the record of them all with the range of the
//   values' closing parenthesis. A bare return returns the results' values,
//   and a body that does not end in a terminating statement does so at its
//   closing brace;
// - its body's statement rows in source order, each with its statement's
//   range. The body ends in a terminating statement: a return, an if
//   statement with an else whose two branches end in terminating statements,
//   a switch with a default arm whose arms all end in terminating
//   statements, or a while loop whose condition is the literal true and whose
//   own body has no break. Statements after a terminating one are still recorded,
//   though they never run.
// The statements:
// - a Local row declares variable `value` of `type`, its `name`, and its
//   `count` operand rows follow, its initial value. The variables are the
//   parameters, then the locals in declaration order;
// - an Assign row stores its `count` operands' value in variable `value` of
//   `type`. A compound assignment's operands read the variable first, then
//   the value, then combine them, since no operand can change a variable;
// - an If row is followed by its `count` condition operands, then the
//   statements of its consequence, then optionally an Else row, the range of
//   its alternative, and the alternative's statements, then an End row with
//   the If row's range. An else-if is an Else row whose one statement is an
//   If;
// - a While row is followed by its `count` condition operands, then the
//   statements of its body, then an End row with the While row's range. The
//   condition runs before each pass;
// - a For row declares variable `value`, an int named `name`, and its
//   `count` operands follow: its first bound's, then its last bound's, each
//   an int, run once before the loop. Its `literal` is the sum of
//   RangeExclusive for `..<` and RangeReverse for `for <`. The statements of
//   its body follow, then an End row with the For row's range. The variable
//   takes each value from the first bound up to the last, the last itself
//   only for `..`, or the same values from the last down, and the body cannot
//   assign it. A bound evaluated once cannot change, so neither end wraps;
// - a Switch row is followed by its `count` operands, its value, of its
//   integer `type`, run once. Then each arm in source order: a Case row with
//   the arm's range and the Switch row's `type`, its `count` operands, the
//   arm's value of that type, or none for the default arm, then the arm's
//   statements. An End row with the Switch row's range follows the last arm.
//   The value arms are tried in order, each value evaluated only when the
//   arms before it missed, and the first arm whose value equals the switch's
//   runs; the default arm, wherever it is, runs when none does. An arm does
//   not fall through into the next. A switch on a union variable is one on
//   its Tag: its operands read the variable, then take its Tag, and each
//   variant arm's value is a Literal of the variant's ordinal, with the range
//   of the pattern's name. Its last arm is its default arm when it has none,
//   since the variable holds one of the variants the arms cover. Each of an
//   arm's bindings, in order, is a Local row with the binding's range whose
//   operands read the variable and take that Payload, with the same range,
//   before the arm's statements;
// - a Break or Continue row leaves, or ends the pass of, the innermost loop
//   it is in, through any switch around it. It has no operands;
// - a Return row is followed by its `count` operands, the returned value;
// - an Assert row, with the range of its assert's call, is followed by its
//   `count` condition operands. When the condition is false, the call from
//   the VM ends with the failure the VM's assert at that range gives, and
//   every call it is in ends with it.
// A variable is visible from the end of its declaration to the end of the
// statements it is declared among; locals of a consequence, an alternative,
// an arm or a loop body are not visible after it, and a For row's variable is
// visible only in its body.
//
// Operand rows are in evaluation order, each after the operands it consumes:
// a Literal's `literal` is its value, or 0 and 1 for false and true, and a
// word's is its bits, as a float's and a double's are their IEEE encodings; a
// string Literal's `text` is its decoded bytes and its `literal` 0;
// a Read's `value` is its variable's ordinal; a Compare's `value` is its
// operator and it consumes the two most recent unconsumed operands, numbers
// of one type, or two bools or two strings for == and !=, left first, to
// give a bool, two strings being equal when their bytes are; an
// Arithmetic's `value` is its operator and it consumes the two most recent
// unconsumed operands, both of its number `type`, left first, to give one of
// that type; a Unary's `value` is its operator and it consumes the most
// recent unconsumed operand of its `type`; a Convert's `value` is Clamp or
// Wrap and it consumes the most recent unconsumed operand, a number its
// `type` converts from, to give one of its `type`; a Logical's `value` is
// LogicalAnd or LogicalOr and it consumes the most recent unconsumed operand,
// its left bool, then its `count` right operand rows follow it, which run
// only when the left does not decide the value, and give a bool, its value;
// a Call's `value` is its callee's function ordinal, `name` its callee's name
// and `count` its arguments, the `count` most recent unconsumed operands in
// argument order; an argument the call omits is a Literal of its parameter's
// default with the call's range. A Zero row is the record of its `type` whose
// fields are all zero; an Update's `value` is a field ordinal of its record
// `type`, and it consumes the two most recent unconsumed operands, a record of
// that type then a value of the field's type, to give the record with that
// field replaced; a Field's `value` is a field ordinal and it consumes the
// most recent unconsumed operand, a record, to give that field, of its
// `type`. A Make's `value` is a variant ordinal of its union `type` and
// `count` that variant's payloads, and it consumes the `count` most recent
// unconsumed operands, the payloads in order, to give the union holding that
// variant; a Tag consumes the most recent unconsumed operand, a union, to give
// its variant's ordinal, an int; a Payload's `value` is a payload ordinal of a
// union and it consumes the most recent unconsumed operand, that union, to
// give the payload, of its `type`. Payloads are read only where their variant
// is held; the backend cannot check this, and another variant's payload reads
// its zero. `v is .V` is the Tag of `v`, a Literal of V's ordinal with the
// pattern's range, then an Equal Compare, or NotEqual for `is not`. A Text
// row reads a string's bytes as the runtime's text natives do, and its
// `value` is its operator: TextLength consumes the most recent unconsumed
// operand, a string, to give its byte count; TextByte consumes the two most
// recent, a string then an index, to give the byte there, 0 to 255, or -1
// outside the string; TextSlice consumes the three most recent, a string,
// then a start and a count, to give the string of those bytes, its start
// clamped to the string and its count to the bytes after the start; and
// TextFormat consumes the `count` most recent, at least one, a string format
// then each argument, a number, bool or string of its own type, to give the
// string format_text makes of them, which the runtime allocates. The
// indexes, starts, counts and byte counts are ints. A record
// literal is a Zero row with the literal's range, then each
// field's value in source order and its Update, with the field's range. An
// assignment to a field, `v.f = x`, is an Assign of `v` whose operands read
// `v`, then `x`, then update `f` with the target's range, and a compound one
// reads `v` and its field `f` before `x` and their combination. An array of
// `T` is type ArrayType + T, for a scalar, a string, or a record or union, and
// no array holds an array. An array is a value: storing one in a variable, or
// returning a parameter's, gives a Copy of it, so an array's elements are
// never shared. An Array row's `value` is its operator.
// ArrayNew consumes its `count` most recent unconsumed operands, elements of
// its array `type`'s element type, to give the array of them in order, the
// empty array for none; ArrayLength consumes an array to give its element
// count, an int; ArrayGet consumes an array and an int index to give the
// element there, of its `type`; ArraySet consumes an array, an int index and
// an element to give the array with that element replaced; ArrayPush consumes
// an array and an element to give the array with the element after its last;
// and ArrayCopy consumes an array to give an independent array of its
// elements. ArrayExtend consumes two arrays of one type to give the first
// followed by the second's elements; ArraySlice consumes an array and an int
// start and count to give a new array of the count elements from the start,
// each clamped to the array; ArrayResize consumes an array and an int length,
// 0 for a negative one, to give a new array of its first elements, or all of
// them, and then as many zeros (false, 0, the empty string, a record of
// zeros) as that length needs; ArrayContains consumes an array of numbers,
// bools or strings and an element of its type to give a bool, whether an
// element equals it, a string by its bytes and a float as IEEE does; and
// ArrayRemove consumes an array and an int index to give the array without
// that element, or the same array for an index outside it. An index of
// ArrayGet or ArraySet outside the array ends the program (D9). ArraySet,
// ArrayPush, ArrayExtend and ArrayRemove change the elements of the array
// they consume, so each is the last operand of an Assign to a variable of the
// array's type whose first operand reads that variable: `a[i] = x` is the
// Assign of `a` with operands that read `a`, then `i`, then `x`, then
// ArraySet, and `a.append(x)` reads `a`, then `x`, then ArrayPush. ArrayWith
// consumes an array and an element, and ArrayJoin two arrays of one type, to
// give a new array of the first's elements and then the second's; neither
// changes what it consumes, as `append(a, x)` and `extend(a, b)` do not. A For row with
// RangeArray declares variable `value`, of its element `type` and `name`,
// and its one operand is the array it iterates, whose elements the body
// cannot change. With RangeKeyed the variable after it is the int index of the
// element, which the body cannot change either. It runs its body for each element in order. The array is
// read once, so a producer iterates a Copy of the variable when the body
// assigns it.
// A condition's last operand is its bool; a
// return's last operand is the function's returned value, and a Local's or an
// Assign's is its variable's. A shift's count is converted to the shifted
// type before the shift consumes it, and an integer operand of floating
// arithmetic, a comparison or a float to double widening has its own Convert
// rows, as the ordinary compiler converts them.
//
// Ranges are half-open source-byte offsets into the source text; lines and
// columns are one-based source-byte positions. Fields a row does not use are
// zero or empty. The rows own their strings: a host snapshot of the array is
// the retained result, not a view into the producer's invocation.
pub Row :: struct {
    kind: int;
    type: int;
    value: int;
    count: int;
    literal: i64;
    name: string;
    text: string;
    start: int;
    end: int;
    line: int;
    column: int;
}

pub SourceRow: int : 0;
pub RejectionRow: int : 1;
pub DocumentRow: int : 2;
pub FunctionRow: int : 3;
pub ParameterRow: int : 4;
pub ReturnRow: int : 5;
pub LiteralRow: int : 6;
pub ReadRow: int : 7;
pub CallRow: int : 8;
pub IfRow: int : 9;
pub CompareRow: int : 10;
pub ArithmeticRow: int : 11;
pub UnaryRow: int : 12;
pub ConvertRow: int : 13;
pub LocalRow: int : 14;
pub AssignRow: int : 15;
pub ElseRow: int : 16;
pub EndRow: int : 17;
pub LogicalRow: int : 18;
pub WhileRow: int : 19;
pub ForRow: int : 20;
pub BreakRow: int : 21;
pub ContinueRow: int : 22;
pub SwitchRow: int : 23;
pub CaseRow: int : 24;
pub RecordRow: int : 25;
pub MemberRow: int : 26;
pub ZeroRow: int : 27;
pub UpdateRow: int : 28;
pub FieldRow: int : 29;
pub UnionRow: int : 30;
pub VariantRow: int : 31;
pub MakeRow: int : 32;
pub TagRow: int : 33;
pub PayloadRow: int : 34;
pub TextRow: int : 35;
pub AssertRow: int : 36;
pub ArrayRow: int : 37;
// A call of a C function the source declares in a foreign block, an operand
// row that consumes its `count` argument operands, numbers or bools. `name`
// is the C symbol, `type` the number or bool it returns, and `text` the path
// of the library that provides it, which the native build links.
pub ForeignRow: int : 38;

// The value types: int and bool, then the other integer types, then the
// floating ones, then string. The facts carry no compiler storage ordinal.
pub IntType: int : 1;
pub BoolType: int : 2;
pub Int8Type: int : 3;
pub ByteType: int : 4;
pub Int16Type: int : 5;
pub Uint16Type: int : 6;
pub UintType: int : 7;
pub Int64Type: int : 8;
pub WordType: int : 9;
pub FloatType: int : 10;
pub DoubleType: int : 11;
// An immutable string of bytes.
pub StringType: int : 12;
// The d-th record or union of the module is type RecordType + d.
pub RecordType: int : 32;
// An array of type T is ArrayType + T.
pub ArrayType: int : 1024;

// Compare operators: the source's <, <=, >, >=, == and !=, signed or
// unsigned as their operands' type is. On floats, each is false when an
// operand is NaN, except != which is true.
pub Less: int : 1;
pub LessEqual: int : 2;
pub Greater: int : 3;
pub GreaterEqual: int : 4;
pub Equal: int : 5;
pub NotEqual: int : 6;

// Arithmetic operators: the source's +, -, *, /, %, &, |, ^, << and >> on
// integers. None fails: they wrap, x / 0 is 0, x % 0 is x, MIN / -1 is MIN
// and MIN % -1 is 0, and a shift count at or past the width, read unsigned,
// gives 0, or the sign fill for a signed >>. Floats have +, -, * and /,
// IEEE at their own width, rounding to nearest.
pub Add: int : 1;
pub Subtract: int : 2;
pub Multiply: int : 3;
pub Divide: int : 4;
pub Remainder: int : 5;
pub And: int : 6;
pub Or: int : 7;
pub Xor: int : 8;
pub ShiftLeft: int : 9;
pub ShiftRight: int : 10;

// Unary operators: the source's - and ~ on integers, and ! on bool;
// negation wraps.
pub Negate: int : 1;
pub Complement: int : 2;
pub Not: int : 3;

// Logical operators: the source's && and ||.
pub LogicalAnd: int : 1;
pub LogicalOr: int : 2;

// Text operators: the runtime's text_byte_length, text_byte_at, text_slice
// and format_text.
pub TextLength: int : 1;
pub TextByte: int : 2;
pub TextSlice: int : 3;
pub TextFormat: int : 4;

// Array operators.
pub ArrayNew: int : 1;
pub ArrayLength: int : 2;
pub ArrayGet: int : 3;
pub ArraySet: int : 4;
pub ArrayPush: int : 5;
pub ArrayCopy: int : 6;
pub ArrayExtend: int : 7;
pub ArraySlice: int : 8;
pub ArrayResize: int : 9;
pub ArrayContains: int : 10;
pub ArrayRemove: int : 11;
pub ArrayWith: int : 12;
pub ArrayJoin: int : 13;
// An array of ints, the bytes of the string it consumes, from 0 to 255.
pub ArrayBytes: int : 14;
// A Local row whose `literal` is SwitchSubject holds the union a switch is on
// when the switch is not on a variable, so each arm's bindings can read it
// again. It has the switch statement's range, a Switch row of the same range
// follows it, and that Switch reads it.
pub SwitchSubject: int : 1;
// A Local row whose `literal` is Retained declares a retained local, a number
// or bool the function keeps between calls: its one operand is a Literal, the
// value it starts with, and the variable reads and writes that state, which a
// native library exports to the host that replaces it. It is named for its
// function and its `name`, so the state is the same across edits that keep both.
pub Retained: int : 2;

// A For row's range flags; RangeArray stands alone or with RangeKeyed, which
// declares the next variable, an int, as the index of the element.
pub RangeExclusive: int : 1;
pub RangeReverse: int : 2;
pub RangeArray: int : 4;
pub RangeKeyed: int : 8;

// Conversions between integer types: `T(value)` clamps the value to T, and
// `T(wrap: value)` keeps its low bits. A floating value converts to an
// integer type in either mode by truncating toward zero, clamping to the
// type, with NaN as 0. An integer converts to double exactly or to nearest,
// a float to double exactly and a double to float to nearest. An integer
// becomes a float only through double, so it has two Convert rows.
pub Clamp: int : 0;
pub Wrap: int : 1;

// The bits of an integer type, 0 for bool or any other.
pub Bits :: (type: int) -> int {
    if (type == Int8Type || type == ByteType) {
        return 8;
    }
    if (type == Int16Type || type == Uint16Type) {
        return 16;
    }
    if (type == IntType || type == UintType) {
        return 32;
    }
    if (type == Int64Type || type == WordType) {
        return 64;
    }
    return 0;
}
// Whether an integer type is unsigned.
pub Unsigned :: (type: int) -> bool {
    return type == ByteType || type == Uint16Type || type == UintType || type == WordType;
}
// Whether a type is float or double.
pub Floating :: (type: int) -> bool {
    return type == FloatType || type == DoubleType;
}
// Whether `literal` is a value of `type`: 0 or 1 for bool, its range for an
// integer type, any bits for a word or double, 32 bits for a float and 0 for
// a string, whose value is its text.
pub Fits :: (literal: i64, type: int) -> bool {
    if (type == StringType) {
        return literal == 0;
    }
    if (type == FloatType) {
        return literal >= 0 && literal < i64(1) << u32(32);
    }
    bits := Bits(type);
    if (bits == 0) {
        return type == DoubleType || (type == BoolType && (literal == 0 || literal == 1));
    }
    if (bits == 64) {
        return true;
    }
    if (Unsigned(type)) {
        return literal >= 0 && literal < i64(1) << u32(bits);
    }
    half := i64(1) << u32(bits - 1);
    return literal >= 0 - half && literal < half;
}
